{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc | sed -n '1,460p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t(ns dao.stream.apply\n     2\t  \"The transport-neutral request/response envelope for DaoStream v2.\n     3\t\n     4\t   This namespace owns only application data and one explicit server step.  It\n     5\t   neither creates streams nor retains handles, cursors, handlers, or requests\n     6\t   outside values supplied by its caller.  A driver owns the returned state and\n     7\t   decides when to call `serve-once!` again.\"\n     8\t  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]))\n     9\t\n    10\t\n    11\t;; =============================================================================\n    12\t;; Envelope\n    13\t;; =============================================================================\n    14\t\n    15\t(def id-key :dao.stream.apply/id)\n    16\t(def op-key :dao.stream.apply/op)\n    17\t(def args-key :dao.stream.apply/args)\n    18\t(def ok-key :dao.stream.apply/ok)\n    19\t(def error-key :dao.stream.apply/error)\n    20\t(def code-key :dao.stream.apply/code)\n    21\t(def message-key :dao.stream.apply/message)\n    22\t\n    23\t\n    24\t(defn correlation-id?\n    25\t  \"True for a usable opaque correlation id.  Allocation policy (including the\n    26\t   cross-host safe-integer bound) belongs to the RPC client, not this envelope.\"\n    27\t  [id]\n    28\t  (some? id))\n    29\t\n    30\t\n    31\t(defn request\n    32\t  \"Construct a request envelope.  Use `request?` to validate data received from\n    33\t   a stream; constructors deliberately add no interpretation to arguments.\"\n    34\t  [id op args]\n    35\t  {id-key id op-key op args-key args})\n    36\t\n    37\t\n    38\t(defn request?\n    39\t  \"True when value is a valid request envelope.  Unknown keys are intentionally\n    40\t   ignored so the envelope remains open.\"\n    41\t  [value]\n    42\t  (and (map? value)\n    43\t       (correlation-id? (get value id-key))\n    44\t       (keyword? (get value op-key))\n    45\t       (vector? (get value args-key))))\n    46\t\n    47\t\n    48\t(defn request-id\n    49\t  [value]\n    50\t  (get value id-key))\n    51\t\n    52\t\n    53\t(defn request-op\n    54\t  [value]\n    55\t  (get value op-key))\n    56\t\n    57\t\n    58\t(defn request-args\n    59\t  [value]\n    60\t  (get value args-key))\n    61\t\n    62\t\n    63\t(defn error\n    64\t  \"Construct portable error data.  Codes are qualified keywords and messages\n    65\t   are ordinary strings, never host exception values.\"\n    66\t  [code message]\n    67\t  {code-key code message-key message})\n    68\t\n    69\t\n    70\t(defn error?\n    71\t  [value]\n    72\t  (and (map? value)\n    73\t       (stream/qualified-keyword?* (get value code-key))\n    74\t       (string? (get value message-key))))\n    75\t\n    76\t\n    77\t(defn success-response\n    78\t  \"Construct a successful response preserving the request id.\"\n    79\t  [id value]\n    80\t  {id-key id ok-key value})\n    81\t\n    82\t\n    83\t(defn error-response\n    84\t  \"Construct an error response preserving the request id.\"\n    85\t  [id code message]\n    86\t  {id-key id error-key (error code message)})\n    87\t\n    88\t\n    89\t(defn response?\n    90\t  \"True when value is a valid response envelope.  A response contains exactly\n    91\t   one of `:dao.stream.apply/ok` or `/error`; nil is a valid success value.\"\n    92\t  [value]\n    93\t  (and (map? value)\n    94\t       (correlation-id? (get value id-key))\n    95\t       (not= (contains? value ok-key) (contains? value error-key))\n    96\t       (or (contains? value ok-key)\n    97\t           (error? (get value error-key)))))\n    98\t\n    99\t\n   100\t(defn response-id\n   101\t  [value]\n   102\t  (get value id-key))\n   103\t\n   104\t\n   105\t(defn response-ok\n   106\t  [value]\n   107\t  (get value ok-key))\n   108\t\n   109\t\n   110\t(defn response-error\n   111\t  [value]\n   112\t  (get value error-key))\n   113\t\n   114\t\n   115\t;; =============================================================================\n   116\t;; Endpoint descriptors and explicit stream helpers\n   117\t;; =============================================================================\n   118\t\n   119\t(def request-key :dao.stream.apply/request)\n   120\t(def response-key :dao.stream.apply/response)\n   121\t\n   122\t\n   123\t(defn endpoint\n   124\t  \"Construct a portable endpoint pair from request and response descriptors.\n   125\t   Resolving descriptors into handles is host composition and stays outside this\n   126\t   namespace.\"\n   127\t  [request-descriptor response-descriptor]\n   128\t  {request-key request-descriptor response-key response-descriptor})\n   129\t\n   130\t\n   131\t(def make-endpoint endpoint)\n   132\t\n   133\t\n   134\t(defn endpoint?\n   135\t  [value]\n   136\t  (and (map? value)\n   137\t       (stream/valid-descriptor? (get value request-key))\n   138\t       (stream/valid-descriptor? (get value response-key))))\n   139\t\n   140\t\n   141\t(defn endpoint-request\n   142\t  [value]\n   143\t  (get value request-key))\n   144\t\n   145\t\n   146\t(defn endpoint-response\n   147\t  [value]\n   148\t  (get value response-key))\n   149\t\n   150\t\n   151\t(defn put-request!\n   152\t  \"Append one validated request to an explicit writer handle.  Invalid envelope\n   153\t   data is reported as application data and is never appended.\"\n   154\t  [request-handle request-value]\n   155\t  (if (request? request-value)\n   156\t    (stream/append! request-handle request-value)\n   157\t    {:dao.stream.apply/outcome :dao.stream.apply/invalid-request\n   158\t     :dao.stream.apply/value request-value}))\n   159\t\n   160\t\n   161\t(defn put-response!\n   162\t  \"Append one validated response to an explicit writer handle.\"\n   163\t  [response-handle response-value]\n   164\t  (if (response? response-value)\n   165\t    (stream/append! response-handle response-value)\n   166\t    {:dao.stream.apply/outcome :dao.stream.apply/invalid-response\n   167\t     :dao.stream.apply/value response-value}))\n   168\t\n   169\t\n   170\t(defn next-request\n   171\t  \"Read one raw request value at the explicit cursor.  Validation belongs to the\n   172\t   server step because malformed stream data must be consumed once, not retried.\"\n   173\t  [request-handle request-cursor]\n   174\t  (stream/next request-handle request-cursor))\n   175\t\n   176\t\n   177\t(defn next-response\n   178\t  \"Read one raw response value at the explicit cursor.\"\n   179\t  [response-handle response-cursor]\n   180\t  (stream/next response-handle response-cursor))\n   181\t\n   182\t\n   183\t;; =============================================================================\n   184\t;; Pure request dispatch\n   185\t;; =============================================================================\n   186\t\n   187\t(defn dispatch-request\n   188\t  \"Invoke at most one handler for a validated request and return its correlated\n   189\t   response data.  Unknown operations and host handler failures are ordinary,\n   190\t   portable error responses; no exception escapes into a polling driver.\"\n   191\t  [handlers request-value]\n   192\t  (cond\n   193\t    (not (request? request-value))\n   194\t    (when (correlation-id? (request-id request-value))\n   195\t      (error-response (request-id request-value)\n   196\t                      :dao.stream.apply/malformed-request\n   197\t                      \"Malformed request envelope\"))\n   198\t\n   199\t    :else\n   200\t    (let [id (request-id request-value)\n   201\t          op (request-op request-value)\n   202\t          handler (get handlers op)]\n   203\t      (if-not (fn? handler)\n   204\t        (error-response id :dao.stream.apply/unknown-operation\n   205\t                        \"No handler for operation\")\n   206\t        (try\n   207\t          (success-response id (apply handler (request-args request-value)))\n   208\t          (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _\n   209\t            (error-response id :dao.stream.apply/handler-error\n   210\t                            \"Handler failed\")))))))\n   211\t\n   212\t\n   213\t;; =============================================================================\n   214\t;; One explicit server step\n   215\t;; =============================================================================\n   216\t\n   217\t(defn server-state\n   218\t  \"Create caller-owned state for `serve-once!`.  The cursor must have been\n   219\t   minted by the request handle; this namespace never fabricates one.\"\n   220\t  [request-cursor]\n   221\t  {:request-cursor request-cursor\n   222\t   :pending-response nil\n   223\t   :pending-request-id nil\n   224\t   :pending-successor nil\n   225\t   :terminal nil\n   226\t   :diagnostics []})\n   227\t\n   228\t\n   229\t(defn- step-result\n   230\t  [outcome state & {:as extra}]\n   231\t  (merge {:dao.stream.apply/outcome outcome\n   232\t          :dao.stream.apply/state state}\n   233\t         extra))\n   234\t\n   235\t\n   236\t(defn- clear-pending\n   237\t  [state]\n   238\t  (assoc state\n   239\t         :pending-response nil\n   240\t         :pending-request-id nil\n   241\t         :pending-successor nil))\n   242\t\n   243\t\n   244\t(defn- retain-response\n   245\t  [state response successor]\n   246\t  (assoc state\n   247\t         :pending-response response\n   248\t         :pending-request-id (response-id response)\n   249\t         :pending-successor successor))\n   250\t\n   251\t\n   252\t(defn- deliver-pending\n   253\t  [response-handle state]\n   254\t  (let [response (:pending-response state)\n   255\t        put-result (put-response! response-handle response)\n   256\t        outcome (:dao.stream/outcome put-result)]\n   257\t    (case outcome\n   258\t      :dao.stream/ok\n   259\t      (step-result :dao.stream.apply/responded\n   260\t                   (-> state\n   261\t                       (assoc :request-cursor (:pending-successor state))\n   262\t                       clear-pending)\n   263\t                   :dao.stream.apply/response response\n   264\t                   :dao.stream.apply/append put-result)\n   265\t\n   266\t      :dao.stream/full\n   267\t      (step-result :dao.stream.apply/pending-response state\n   268\t                   :dao.stream.apply/response response\n   269\t                   :dao.stream.apply/append put-result)\n   270\t\n   271\t      ;; A response that cannot be delivered is consumed once and terminal for\n   272\t      ;; this binding.  Retrying would rerun neither handler nor cursor, but\n   273\t      ;; would conceal the known loss from the caller.\n   274\t      (:dao.stream/invalid-value :dao.stream/closed :dao.stream/transport-error)\n   275\t      (step-result :dao.stream.apply/response-undeliverable\n   276\t                   (-> state\n   277\t                       (assoc :request-cursor (:pending-successor state)\n   278\t                              :terminal outcome)\n   279\t                       clear-pending)\n   280\t                   :dao.stream.apply/response response\n   281\t                   :dao.stream.apply/append put-result)\n   282\t\n   283\t      ;; A helper-level invalid response is also terminal.  It indicates a\n   284\t      ;; caller/handler defect but still must not leave a request poison-pill.\n   285\t      (step-result :dao.stream.apply/response-undeliverable\n   286\t                   (-> state\n   287\t                       (assoc :request-cursor (:pending-successor state)\n   288\t                              :terminal outcome)\n   289\t                       clear-pending)\n   290\t                   :dao.stream.apply/response response\n   291\t                   :dao.stream.apply/append put-result))))\n   292\t\n   293\t\n   294\t(defn- terminal-result\n   295\t  [state outcome read-result]\n   296\t  (step-result :dao.stream.apply/terminal\n   297\t               (assoc state :terminal outcome)\n   298\t               :dao.stream.apply/read read-result))\n   299\t\n   300\t\n   301\t(defn serve-once!\n   302\t  \"Advance one server session by at most one request and one response append.\n   303\t\n   304\t   `state` is explicit caller-owned data.  A computed response and its exact\n   305\t   successor cursor remain in that state until append succeeds, so retrying on\n   306\t   `:dao.stream/full` cannot invoke the handler twice.  This function neither\n   307\t   loops nor waits.\"\n   308\t  [handlers request-handle response-handle state]\n   309\t  (cond\n   310\t    (:terminal state)\n   311\t    (step-result :dao.stream.apply/terminal state)\n   312\t\n   313\t    (:pending-response state)\n   314\t    (deliver-pending response-handle state)\n   315\t\n   316\t    :else\n   317\t    (let [read-result (next-request request-handle (:request-cursor state))\n   318\t          outcome (:dao.stream/outcome read-result)]\n   319\t      (case outcome\n   320\t        :dao.stream/ok\n   321\t        (let [request-value (:dao.stream/value read-result)\n   322\t              successor (:dao.stream/cursor read-result)]\n   323\t          (if-let [response (dispatch-request handlers request-value)]\n   324\t            (deliver-pending response-handle\n   325\t                             (retain-response state response successor))\n   326\t            ;; A malformed element with no usable id cannot be correlated, but\n   327\t            ;; it still advances exactly once and becomes a local diagnostic.\n   328\t            (step-result :dao.stream.apply/malformed-request\n   329\t                         (-> state\n   330\t                             (assoc :request-cursor successor)\n   331\t                             (update :diagnostics conj\n   332\t                                     {:dao.stream.apply/code\n   333\t                                      :dao.stream.apply/malformed-request\n   334\t                                      :dao.stream.apply/value request-value}))\n   335\t                         :dao.stream.apply/read read-result)))\n   336\t\n   337\t        :dao.stream/blocked\n   338\t        (step-result :dao.stream.apply/idle state\n   339\t                     :dao.stream.apply/read read-result)\n   340\t\n   341\t        :dao.stream/gap\n   342\t        (step-result :dao.stream.apply/gap\n   343\t                     (-> state\n   344\t                         (assoc :request-cursor (:dao.stream/cursor read-result))\n   345\t                         (update :diagnostics conj\n   346\t                                 {:dao.stream.apply/code\n   347\t                                  :dao.stream.apply/request-gap}))\n   348\t                     :dao.stream.apply/read read-result)\n   349\t\n   350\t        (:dao.stream/end :dao.stream/cursor-mismatch :dao.stream/invalid-cursor\n   351\t                         :dao.stream/transport-error)\n   352\t        (terminal-result state outcome read-result)\n   353\t\n   354\t        ;; A malformed transport implementation is terminal at this consumer;\n   355\t        ;; retrying an unknown answer could spin forever.\n   356\t        (terminal-result state outcome read-result)))))\n     1\t(ns dao.stream.rpc\n     2\t  \"Transport-neutral, polling RPC state machines for DaoStream v2.\n     3\t\n     4\t   This layer deliberately owns no socket, scheduler, atom, promise, callback,\n     5\t   retry policy, or transport registry.  A composition supplies two explicit\n     6\t   handles (a request writer and a response reader), owns the returned state,\n     7\t   and chooses when to step `request!` and `poll!`.\n     8\t\n     9\t   The wire values are `dao.stream.apply` envelopes: a request is\n    10\t   `{:dao.stream.apply/id :dao.stream.apply/op :dao.stream.apply/args}` and an\n    11\t   answer is `{:dao.stream.apply/id :dao.stream.apply/ok <value>}` or\n    12\t   `{:dao.stream.apply/id :dao.stream.apply/error {:dao.stream.apply/code\n    13\t   :dao.stream.apply/message}}`.  Envelopes stay open and are retained and\n    14\t   forwarded whole.  Events, diagnostics, and completions are this client's\n    15\t   own state and keep the `:dao.stream.rpc/*` keys; only the request and\n    16\t   answer values they embed are apply envelopes.\n    17\t\n    18\t   Id allocation is client policy layered over apply's opaque correlation id:\n    19\t   ids are self-minted random safe integers, not a sequential counter, so\n    20\t   callers sharing one answers stream must not collide on a caller-chosen id\n    21\t   (docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md, section 5).  A decoded answer whose id\n    22\t   is not `safe-id?` is a diagnostic here, not an apply-level rejection.  The\n    23\t   number of outstanding requests is bounded by `:max-outstanding`.\"\n    24\t  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]\n    25\t            [dao.stream.apply :as apply2]))\n    26\t\n    27\t\n    28\t;; =============================================================================\n    29\t;; Public data vocabulary\n    30\t;; =============================================================================\n    31\t\n    32\t(def max-safe-id\n    33\t  \"Largest integer that remains exactly representable by every supported host.\"\n    34\t  9007199254740991)\n    35\t\n    36\t\n    37\t(def event-key :dao.stream.rpc/event)\n    38\t(def response-key :dao.stream.rpc/response)\n    39\t(def diagnostic-key :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic)\n    40\t(def code-key :dao.stream.rpc/code)\n    41\t\n    42\t\n    43\t(def max-id-retries\n    44\t  \"Bound on re-rolling a self-minted id after a collision, before allocation\n    45\t   fails through the existing terminal path.\"\n    46\t  8)\n    47\t\n    48\t\n    49\t(def default-max-outstanding\n    50\t  \"Outstanding-request limit used when `client-state` is not given one.\"\n    51\t  64)\n    52\t\n    53\t\n    54\t(defn response-event\n    55\t  [response]\n    56\t  {event-key :dao.stream.rpc/response response-key response})\n    57\t\n    58\t\n    59\t(defn diagnostic-event\n    60\t  ([code]\n    61\t   (diagnostic-event code nil))\n    62\t  ([code value]\n    63\t   {event-key :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic\n    64\t    diagnostic-key {code-key code\n    65\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/value value}}))\n    66\t\n    67\t\n    68\t(defn ignore-event\n    69\t  []\n    70\t  {event-key :dao.stream.rpc/ignore})\n    71\t\n    72\t\n    73\t(defn safe-id?\n    74\t  \"True for an integer which round-trips without precision loss across CLJ,\n    75\t   CLJS, and CLJD.  IDs are routing tokens, not application values.\"\n    76\t  [id]\n    77\t  (and (integer? id) (<= 0 id max-safe-id)))\n    78\t\n    79\t\n    80\t;; =============================================================================\n    81\t;; Request/answer values: thin aliases of the dao.stream.apply envelope\n    82\t;; =============================================================================\n    83\t\n    84\t(def request-value apply2/request)\n    85\t(def request-value? apply2/request?)\n    86\t(def request-id apply2/request-id)\n    87\t(def request-op apply2/request-op)\n    88\t(def request-args apply2/request-args)\n    89\t(def success-answer apply2/success-response)\n    90\t(def error-answer apply2/error-response)\n    91\t(def answer-id apply2/response-id)\n    92\t(def answer-ok apply2/response-ok)\n    93\t(def answer-error apply2/response-error)\n    94\t\n    95\t\n    96\t(defn answer?\n    97\t  \"True for an apply response this client can correlate: `apply/response?`\n    98\t   (which validates an error body) plus this client's `safe-id?` policy.\"\n    99\t  [v]\n   100\t  (and (apply2/response? v) (safe-id? (apply2/response-id v))))\n   101\t\n   102\t\n   103\t(defn answer-ok?\n   104\t  [answer]\n   105\t  (contains? answer apply2/ok-key))\n   106\t\n   107\t\n   108\t;; =============================================================================\n   109\t;; Explicit client state\n   110\t;; =============================================================================\n   111\t\n   112\t(defn- random-safe-id\n   113\t  []\n   114\t  #?(:clj (long (* max-safe-id (rand)))\n   115\t     :cljs (Math/floor (* max-safe-id (rand)))\n   116\t     :cljd (.floor ^double (* max-safe-id (rand)))))\n   117\t\n   118\t\n   119\t(defn client-state\n   120\t  \"Create caller-owned RPC client state.\n   121\t\n   122\t   `writer` is the request path. `reader` and `response-cursor` are the\n   123\t   independent response path.  No cursor is fabricated here; a caller\n   124\t   over a dao.stream.remote reflection passes `stream/anchor-newest`\n   125\t   (the request/response service's own mint, docs/design/\n   126\t   dao.stream.remote.md section 5) and `poll!` resolves it, retrying\n   127\t   while the reflection answers a retryable mint -- the same pattern\n   128\t   `dao.jing.content.step/mint-cursor` uses for the content client.\n   129\t\n   130\t   `opts` may carry `:max-outstanding`, a positive integer bounding accepted\n   131\t   requests awaiting an answer (default `default-max-outstanding`).  At the\n   132\t   limit `request!` answers `:dao.stream.rpc/backpressure` without allocating\n   133\t   an id or appending.\"\n   134\t  ([writer reader response-cursor]\n   135\t   (client-state writer reader response-cursor {}))\n   136\t  ([writer reader response-cursor opts]\n   137\t   (let [limit (get opts :max-outstanding default-max-outstanding)]\n   138\t     (when-not (and (integer? limit) (pos? limit))\n   139\t       (throw (ex-info \"max-outstanding must be a positive integer\"\n   140\t                       {:dao.stream.rpc/max-outstanding limit})))\n   141\t     {:writer writer\n   142\t      :reader reader\n   143\t      :cursor response-cursor\n   144\t      :max-outstanding limit\n   145\t      :outstanding {}\n   146\t      :unsent nil\n   147\t      :completed []\n   148\t      :diagnostics []\n   149\t      :terminal nil})))\n   150\t\n   151\t\n   152\t(def init-client client-state)\n   153\t\n   154\t\n   155\t(defn- rpc-result\n   156\t  [outcome state & {:as extra}]\n   157\t  (merge {:dao.stream.rpc/outcome outcome\n   158\t          :dao.stream.rpc/state state}\n   159\t         extra))\n   160\t\n   161\t\n   162\t(defn- valid-operation-result\n   163\t  [op result]\n   164\t  (if (stream/valid-outcome? op result)\n   165\t    result\n   166\t    {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\n   167\t     :dao.stream/error :dao.stream/invalid-operation-result}))\n   168\t\n   169\t\n   170\t(defn- completion\n   171\t  [request & {:keys [response reason]}]\n   172\t  (cond-> {:dao.stream.rpc/id (:id request)\n   173\t           :dao.stream.rpc/op (:op request)\n   174\t           :dao.stream.rpc/args (:args request)}\n   175\t    response (assoc :dao.stream.rpc/response response)\n   176\t    reason (assoc :dao.stream.rpc/reason reason)))\n   177\t\n   178\t\n   179\t(defn- append-completion\n   180\t  [state request & {:as fields}]\n   181\t  (update state :completed conj (apply completion request (mapcat identity fields))))\n   182\t\n   183\t\n   184\t(defn- append-diagnostic\n   185\t  [state code value]\n   186\t  (update state :diagnostics conj {code-key code\n   187\t                                   :dao.stream.rpc/value value}))\n   188\t\n   189\t\n   190\t(defn- ids-in-use?\n   191\t  [state id]\n   192\t  (or (= id (get-in state [:unsent :id]))\n   193\t      (contains? (:outstanding state) id)\n   194\t      (boolean (some #(= id (:dao.stream.rpc/id %)) (:completed state)))))\n   195\t\n   196\t\n   197\t;; Defined below with the other completion bookkeeping; allocation failure is\n   198\t;; a terminal transition and owes the same conservative loss as every other.\n   199\t(declare lose-outstanding)\n   200\t\n   201\t\n   202\t(defn- allocation-failure\n   203\t  [state code]\n   204\t  (let [state (-> state\n   205\t                  (lose-outstanding :dao.stream.rpc/allocator-error true)\n   206\t                  (append-diagnostic code nil))]\n   207\t    (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/allocator-error state\n   208\t                :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic (last (:diagnostics state)))))\n   209\t\n   210\t\n   211\t(defn- allocate-request\n   212\t  \"Self-mint a random safe id in [0, max-safe-id], re-rolling on a collision\n   213\t   with an id already in use by this state, up to `max-id-retries` times.\n   214\t   This is the request/answer service's own id space (docs/design/\n   215\t   dao.stream.remote.md section 5): callers sharing one answers stream must\n   216\t   not collide on a caller-chosen id, so the id is not a sequential counter.\"\n   217\t  [state op args]\n   218\t  (loop [attempt 0]\n   219\t    (if (>= attempt max-id-retries)\n   220\t      (allocation-failure state :dao.stream.rpc/id-collision)\n   221\t      (let [id (random-safe-id)]\n   222\t        (if (ids-in-use? state id)\n   223\t          (recur (inc attempt))\n   224\t          (let [request {:id id\n   225\t                         :op op\n   226\t                         :args args\n   227\t                         :encoded (request-value id op args)}]\n   228\t            (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/allocated\n   229\t                        (assoc state :unsent request))))))))\n   230\t\n   231\t\n   232\t(defn- accept-unsent\n   233\t  [state request]\n   234\t  (-> state\n   235\t      (assoc :unsent nil)\n   236\t      (assoc-in [:outstanding (:id request)]\n   237\t                {:op (:op request) :args (:args request)})))\n   238\t\n   239\t\n   240\t(defn- attempt-unsent\n   241\t  [state]\n   242\t  (let [request (:unsent state)\n   243\t        append-result (valid-operation-result\n   244\t                        :append!\n   245\t                        (stream/append! (:writer state) (:encoded request)))\n   246\t        outcome (:dao.stream/outcome append-result)]\n   247\t    (case outcome\n   248\t      :dao.stream/ok\n   249\t      (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/requested\n   250\t                  (accept-unsent state request)\n   251\t                  :dao.stream.rpc/id (:id request)\n   252\t                  :dao.stream.rpc/append append-result)\n   253\t\n   254\t      :dao.stream/full\n   255\t      (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/pending-request state\n   256\t                  :dao.stream.rpc/id (:id request)\n   257\t                  :dao.stream.rpc/append append-result)\n   258\t\n   259\t      (:dao.stream/closed :dao.stream/invalid-value :dao.stream/transport-error)\n   260\t      (let [state (-> state\n   261\t                      (assoc :unsent nil)\n   262\t                      (append-completion request :reason outcome))]\n   263\t        (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/request-undeliverable state\n   264\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/id (:id request)\n   265\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/reason outcome\n   266\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/append append-result))\n   267\t\n   268\t      ;; A malformed writer answer becomes transport-error in\n   269\t      ;; valid-operation-result, so this branch is defensive only.\n   270\t      (let [state (-> state\n   271\t                      (assoc :unsent nil)\n   272\t                      (append-completion request :reason :dao.stream/transport-error))]\n   273\t        (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/request-undeliverable state\n   274\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/id (:id request)\n   275\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/reason :dao.stream/transport-error\n   276\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/append append-result)))))\n   277\t\n   278\t\n   279\t(defn request!\n   280\t  \"Attempt one request append, returning an explicit next state.\n   281\t\n   282\t   If a previous append was full, this retries the exact already-allocated\n   283\t   envelope and ignores `op`/`args`; it never allocates a replacement id.  The\n   284\t   function makes one append attempt at most and never spins on `:full`.\n   285\t\n   286\t   With `:max-outstanding` requests already outstanding, a new request answers\n   287\t   `:dao.stream.rpc/backpressure`: no id is allocated and nothing is appended.\"\n   288\t  [state op args]\n   289\t  (cond\n   290\t    (:terminal state)\n   291\t    (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/terminal state)\n   292\t\n   293\t    (:unsent state)\n   294\t    (attempt-unsent state)\n   295\t\n   296\t    (not (and (keyword? op) (vector? args)))\n   297\t    (let [state (append-diagnostic state :dao.stream.rpc/invalid-request\n   298\t                                   {:op op :args args})]\n   299\t      (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/invalid-request state\n   300\t                  :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic (last (:diagnostics state))))\n   301\t\n   302\t    (>= (count (:outstanding state)) (:max-outstanding state))\n   303\t    (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/backpressure state)\n   304\t\n   305\t    :else\n   306\t    (let [allocated (allocate-request state op args)]\n   307\t      (if (= :dao.stream.rpc/allocated (:dao.stream.rpc/outcome allocated))\n   308\t        (attempt-unsent (:dao.stream.rpc/state allocated))\n   309\t        allocated))))\n   310\t\n   311\t\n   312\t(defn unsent?\n   313\t  \"True while an allocated request is still owed one accepted append.  A caller\n   314\t   that allocates ids from this state must not submit new work while it holds:\n   315\t   `request!` retries the unsent envelope and ignores the operation it is given.\"\n   316\t  [state]\n   317\t  (some? (:unsent state)))\n   318\t\n   319\t\n   320\t(defn abandon-unsent\n   321\t  \"Give up on an allocated-but-unsent request, appending its completion with\n   322\t   `reason`.\n   323\t\n   324\t   A composition that changes what the writer *is* — an operator disconnect, a\n   325\t   rebind onto a fresh attachment — owns the decision that the retained\n   326\t   envelope no longer belongs to the new binding.  Abandoning it here reports\n   327\t   the loss on the ordinary completion path, so it is neither silently dropped\n   328\t   nor resent in place of the caller's next request.\"\n   329\t  ([state] (abandon-unsent state :dao.stream.rpc/abandoned))\n   330\t  ([state reason]\n   331\t   (if-let [request (:unsent state)]\n   332\t     (-> state\n   333\t         (assoc :unsent nil)\n   334\t         (append-completion request :reason reason))\n   335\t     state)))\n   336\t\n   337\t\n   338\t;; =============================================================================\n   339\t;; Response decoding and correlation\n   340\t;; =============================================================================\n   341\t\n   342\t(defn- answer-diagnostic\n   343\t  \"Diagnostic for a value that is not an answer this client accepts: an apply\n   344\t   response with an id outside `safe-id?` is `unsafe-response-id`; anything\n   345\t   else, including an invalid error body, is `malformed-response`.\"\n   346\t  [value]\n   347\t  (diagnostic-event (if (apply2/response? value)\n   348\t                      :dao.stream.rpc/unsafe-response-id\n   349\t                      :dao.stream.rpc/malformed-response)\n   350\t                    value))\n   351\t\n   352\t\n   353\t(defn- normalize-event\n   354\t  [value]\n   355\t  (cond\n   356\t    (nil? value) (ignore-event)\n   357\t    (answer? value) (response-event value)\n   358\t    (and (map? value) (keyword? (get value event-key))) value\n   359\t    :else (answer-diagnostic value)))\n   360\t\n   361\t\n   362\t(defn- decode-value\n   363\t  [_state value]\n   364\t  (try\n   365\t    (normalize-event value)\n   366\t    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _\n   367\t      (diagnostic-event :dao.stream.rpc/decode-error value))))\n   368\t\n   369\t\n   370\t(defn- lose-outstanding\n   371\t  [state reason terminal?]\n   372\t  (let [state (reduce (fn [s [id request]]\n   373\t                        (append-completion s (assoc request :id id) :reason reason))\n   374\t                      state\n   375\t                      (:outstanding state))]\n   376\t    (cond-> (assoc state :outstanding {})\n   377\t      terminal? (assoc :terminal reason))))\n   378\t\n   379\t\n   380\t(defn handle-event\n   381\t  \"Apply one decoded RPC event to explicit client state.\n   382\t\n   383\t   `response-event`, `diagnostic-event`, and `ignore-event` are the events a\n   384\t   composition constructs; a bare answer value is also accepted.  This\n   385\t   function has no cursor operation and is useful for testing adapters\n   386\t   independently from a response medium.\"\n   387\t  [state event]\n   388\t  (let [event (normalize-event event)\n   389\t        kind (get event event-key)]\n   390\t    (cond\n   391\t      ;; A terminal binding cannot complete an old request later.  Keep the\n   392\t      ;; event as diagnostic data rather than silently treating it as a reply.\n   393\t      (:terminal state)\n   394\t      (let [state (append-diagnostic state :dao.stream.rpc/event-after-terminal event)]\n   395\t        (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic state\n   396\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic (last (:diagnostics state))))\n   397\t\n   398\t      (= kind :dao.stream.rpc/ignore)\n   399\t      (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/ignored state)\n   400\t\n   401\t      (= kind :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic)\n   402\t      (let [diagnostic (get event diagnostic-key)\n   403\t            state (append-diagnostic state (get diagnostic code-key) diagnostic)]\n   404\t        (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic state\n   405\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic (last (:diagnostics state))))\n   406\t\n   407\t      (= kind :dao.stream.rpc/response)\n   408\t      (let [response (get event response-key)]\n   409\t        (if-not (answer? response)\n   410\t          (handle-event state (answer-diagnostic response))\n   411\t          (let [id (answer-id response)]\n   412\t            (if-let [request (get (:outstanding state) id)]\n   413\t              (let [state (-> state\n   414\t                              (update :outstanding dissoc id)\n   415\t                              (append-completion (assoc request :id id)\n   416\t                                                 :response response))]\n   417\t                (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/responded state\n   418\t                            :dao.stream.rpc/id id\n   419\t                            :dao.stream.rpc/response response))\n   420\t              (handle-event state\n   421\t                            (diagnostic-event :dao.stream.rpc/unsolicited-response response))))))\n   422\t\n   423\t      :else\n   424\t      (handle-event state (diagnostic-event :dao.stream.rpc/unhandled-event event)))))\n   425\t\n   426\t\n   427\t(defn- transport-error-reason\n   428\t  \"Translate a reflection's `:dao.stream/transport-error` into the client\n   429\t   terminal vocabulary, inspecting `:dao.stream.remote/reason` when the\n   430\t   reflection supplied one (dao.stream.remote.cljc reasons a reflection's\n   431\t   read failure this way).  A reason this translation does not recognize\n   432\t   still becomes the generic transport-error terminal, never left raw.\n   433\t\n   434\t   Each remote reason keeps its own word: `no-surface` and `oversize` are not\n   435\t   collapsed into `transport-error`.  Only `detached` is rebindable (`rebind`);\n   436\t   every other reason is terminal for the binding.\"\n   437\t  [read-result]\n   438\t  (case (:dao.stream.remote/reason read-result)\n   439\t    :dao.stream.remote/not-found :dao.stream.apply/not-found\n   440\t    :dao.stream.remote/channel-gone :dao.stream.apply/detached\n   441\t    :dao.stream.remote/no-surface :dao.stream.apply/no-surface\n   442\t    :dao.stream.remote/oversize :dao.stream.apply/oversize\n   443\t    :dao.stream.apply/transport-error))\n   444\t\n   445\t\n   446\t(defn- terminal-lost\n   447\t  \"Translate a non-ok, non-blocked, non-gap read or mint result into the\n   448\t   client's terminal vocabulary and complete every outstanding request\n   449\t   with it.  Shared by `poll-read`'s `next` failures and `mint-cursor`'s\n   450\t   terminal mint failures, so a reasoned not-found or channel-gone\n   451\t   reaches the terminal the same way regardless of which operation\n   452\t   the reflection answered it to.\"\n   453\t  [state result]\n   454\t  (let [reason (case (:dao.stream/outcome result)\n   455\t                 :dao.stream/end :dao.stream.apply/ended\n   456\t                 :dao.stream/transport-error (transport-error-reason result)\n   457\t                 :dao.stream.apply/transport-error)\n   458\t        state (lose-outstanding state reason true)]\n   459\t    (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/lost state\n   460\t                :dao.stream.rpc/reason reason\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc | sed -n '446,650p'; rg -n 'dao\\\\.stream\\\\.apply/|dao\\\\.stream\\\\.remote/|cursor-pending|anchor|pending' src/cljc/yin/repl/{connect,driver,serve,adapter}.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/*.cljc docs/design/dao.stream.md collab/1790575143000-architect-one-envelope-ruling.gpt-6-sol.findings.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc | sed -n '446,650p'; rg -n 'dao\\\\.stream\\\\.apply/|dao\\\\.stream\\\\.remote/|cursor-pending|anchor|pending' src/cljc/yin/repl/{connect,driver,serve,adapter}.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/*.cljc docs/design/dao.stream.md collab/1790575143000-architect-one-envelope-ruling.gpt-6-sol.findings.md\"","aggregated_output":"   446\t(defn- terminal-lost\n   447\t  \"Translate a non-ok, non-blocked, non-gap read or mint result into the\n   448\t   client's terminal vocabulary and complete every outstanding request\n   449\t   with it.  Shared by `poll-read`'s `next` failures and `mint-cursor`'s\n   450\t   terminal mint failures, so a reasoned not-found or channel-gone\n   451\t   reaches the terminal the same way regardless of which operation\n   452\t   the reflection answered it to.\"\n   453\t  [state result]\n   454\t  (let [reason (case (:dao.stream/outcome result)\n   455\t                 :dao.stream/end :dao.stream.apply/ended\n   456\t                 :dao.stream/transport-error (transport-error-reason result)\n   457\t                 :dao.stream.apply/transport-error)\n   458\t        state (lose-outstanding state reason true)]\n   459\t    (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/lost state\n   460\t                :dao.stream.rpc/reason reason\n   461\t                :dao.stream.rpc/read result)))\n   462\t\n   463\t\n   464\t(defn- mint-cursor\n   465\t  \"Resolve an anchor-keyword cursor through the reader.  A retryable\n   466\t   answer -- `:dao.stream/transport-error` carrying `:dao.stream/retry?\n   467\t   true` -- leaves the anchor in place for a later poll (dao.jing.content.\n   468\t   step/mint-cursor is the precedent this mirrors).  Any other non-ok\n   469\t   outcome is a reasoned terminal failure: a reflection can answer\n   470\t   `not-found` or `channel-gone` while minting a cursor\n   471\t   (dao.stream.remote.cljc's `refl-cursor`), and that must not be\n   472\t   mistaken for still-pending.  Returns `[status cursor result]`, status\n   473\t   one of `:ok`, `:retry`, `:terminal`; `result` is retained for\n   474\t   diagnostics either way.\"\n   475\t  [reader anchor]\n   476\t  (let [r (stream/cursor reader anchor)\n   477\t        outcome (:dao.stream/outcome r)]\n   478\t    (cond\n   479\t      (= :dao.stream/ok outcome) [:ok (:dao.stream/cursor r) r]\n   480\t\n   481\t      (and (= :dao.stream/transport-error outcome) (:dao.stream/retry? r))\n   482\t      [:retry anchor r]\n   483\t\n   484\t      :else [:terminal anchor r])))\n   485\t\n   486\t\n   487\t(defn- poll-read\n   488\t  [state]\n   489\t  (let [read-result (valid-operation-result :next (stream/next (:reader state) (:cursor state)))\n   490\t        outcome (:dao.stream/outcome read-result)]\n   491\t    (case outcome\n   492\t      :dao.stream/ok\n   493\t      ;; Advance before decoding. A malformed or unsolicited element is thereby\n   494\t      ;; consumed exactly once and cannot poison polling.\n   495\t      (handle-event (assoc state :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor read-result))\n   496\t                    (decode-value state (:dao.stream/value read-result)))\n   497\t\n   498\t      :dao.stream/blocked\n   499\t      (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/idle state :dao.stream.rpc/read read-result)\n   500\t\n   501\t      :dao.stream/gap\n   502\t      (let [state (-> state\n   503\t                      (assoc :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor read-result))\n   504\t                      (lose-outstanding :dao.stream/gap false))]\n   505\t        (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/lost state\n   506\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/reason :dao.stream/gap\n   507\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/read read-result))\n   508\t\n   509\t      (terminal-lost state read-result))))\n   510\t\n   511\t\n   512\t(defn poll!\n   513\t  \"Read at most `budget` response-medium elements.\n   514\t\n   515\t   A `:dao.stream/blocked` result yields immediately; this function never loops\n   516\t   waiting for a response.  Each successful read installs the exact successor\n   517\t   cursor returned by that handle before decoding the element.\"\n   518\t  ([state] (poll! state 1))\n   519\t  ([state budget]\n   520\t   (cond\n   521\t     (:terminal state) (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/terminal state)\n   522\t     (not (and (integer? budget) (pos? budget)))\n   523\t     (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/idle state)\n   524\t     :else\n   525\t     (loop [remaining budget\n   526\t            state state\n   527\t            last-result nil]\n   528\t       (cond\n   529\t         (zero? remaining) (or last-result (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/idle state))\n   530\t\n   531\t         ;; The response cursor is an unresolved anchor: settling it is not\n   532\t         ;; a read and does not spend the budget, exactly as\n   533\t         ;; dao.jing.content.step/poll's own anchor branch does not.\n   534\t         (contains? stream/standard-anchors (:cursor state))\n   535\t         (let [[status cursor r] (mint-cursor (:reader state) (:cursor state))]\n   536\t           (case status\n   537\t             :ok (recur remaining (assoc state :cursor cursor) last-result)\n   538\t             :retry (or last-result\n   539\t                        (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/idle state :dao.stream.rpc/read r))\n   540\t             :terminal (terminal-lost state r)))\n   541\t\n   542\t         :else\n   543\t         (let [result (poll-read state)\n   544\t               next-state (:dao.stream.rpc/state result)\n   545\t               outcome (:dao.stream.rpc/outcome result)]\n   546\t           (if (or (:terminal next-state)\n   547\t                   (= outcome :dao.stream.rpc/idle))\n   548\t             result\n   549\t             (recur (dec remaining) next-state result))))))))\n   550\t\n   551\t\n   552\t(defn take-completed\n   553\t  \"Return `[completions next-state]`, clearing the unpublished completion\n   554\t   outbox exactly once.  The caller owns publication and must call this only\n   555\t   from its single state-owning driver step.\"\n   556\t  [state]\n   557\t  [(:completed state) (assoc state :completed [])])\n   558\t\n   559\t\n   560\t(defn take-diagnostics\n   561\t  \"Return `[diagnostics next-state]`, clearing the unpublished diagnostic outbox\n   562\t   exactly once.  Diagnostics are bounded by publication exactly as completions\n   563\t   are: a noisy shared response medium can produce one per read, so a caller\n   564\t   that never publishes would grow this vector for the session's lifetime.\"\n   565\t  [state]\n   566\t  [(:diagnostics state) (assoc state :diagnostics [])])\n   567\t\n   568\t\n   569\t(defn rebind\n   570\t  \"Replace a detached client's writer (and optionally reader) without creating\n   571\t   a cursor or replacing the random id allocator. Only `/detached` is\n   572\t   reconnectable; other terminal reasons remain terminal.\"\n   573\t  ([state writer]\n   574\t   (if (= :dao.stream.apply/detached (:terminal state))\n   575\t     (assoc state :writer writer :terminal nil)\n   576\t     state))\n   577\t  ([state writer reader]\n   578\t   (if (= :dao.stream.apply/detached (:terminal state))\n   579\t     (assoc state :writer writer :reader reader :terminal nil)\n   580\t     state)))\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/driver.cljc:198:  \"True while the RPC client's response cursor is still the `newest` anchor\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/driver.cljc:199:   `connect/open` composed it with.  Over a remote reflection the anchor is\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/driver.cljc:205:  (contains? stream/standard-anchors (:cursor (rpc-client state))))\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/driver.cljc:208:(defn pending-write?\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/driver.cljc:212:   owner's `moved?` — a pending write must never wait out a backoff ceiling,\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/driver.cljc:227:  #{:dao.stream.apply/detached})\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/driver.cljc:400:      :yin.repl.adapter/pending-request\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/connect.cljc:307:  [handle anchor]\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/connect.cljc:308:  (let [result (stream/cursor handle anchor)]\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/connect.cljc:355:        cursor (mint traffic stream/anchor-newest)\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/connect.cljc:389:   anchor; `rpc/poll!` resolves it, retrying while the answers reflection\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/connect.cljc:425:                                stream/anchor-newest)})))))))))\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/connect.cljc:481:  (= :dao.stream.apply/detached (:terminal client)))\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/connect.cljc:533:    :dao.stream.apply/detached\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/connect.cljc:539:    :dao.stream.apply/ended\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/connect.cljc:544:    :dao.stream.apply/not-found\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/connect.cljc:551:    :dao.stream.apply/transport-error\ncollab/1790575143000-architect-one-envelope-ruling.gpt-6-sol.findings.md:8:There are two corrections to the proposed direction. First, the present rpc client has random safe ID allocation and tracks outstanding requests, but it has **no numeric cap on the outstanding map**: `request!` allocates whenever there is no retained unsent request, and acceptance adds an entry to `:outstanding` without a size check ([rpc.cljc:212](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:212), [rpc.cljc:233](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:233), [rpc.cljc:254](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:254), [rpc.cljc:301](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:301)). Add an explicit, caller supplied positive outstanding limit, with a documented finite default. At the limit, return a local backpressure outcome without allocating an ID or appending a request. Second, “plain apply envelopes” describes the **values on the request and response streams**; the lower `dao.stream.remote` protocol still wraps stream operations in its own id, identity, and outcome envelope ([remote.cljc:48](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:48), [remote.cljc:138](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:138)). It must carry the apply value verbatim.\ncollab/1790575143000-architect-one-envelope-ruling.gpt-6-sol.findings.md:14:| Request | `{:dao.stream.apply/id id :dao.stream.apply/op keyword :dao.stream.apply/args vector}`, with arbitrary additional keys. Use `apply/request?` on received values; preserve the entire map when forwarding or retaining it. This is the existing open contract ([apply.cljc:31](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:31), [apply.cljc:38](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:38)). |\ncollab/1790575143000-architect-one-envelope-ruling.gpt-6-sol.findings.md:15:| Success | `{:dao.stream.apply/id id :dao.stream.apply/ok value}`; `nil` is a valid value ([apply.cljc:77](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:77), [apply.cljc:89](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:89)). |\ncollab/1790575143000-architect-one-envelope-ruling.gpt-6-sol.findings.md:16:| Error | `{:dao.stream.apply/id id :dao.stream.apply/error {:dao.stream.apply/code qualified-keyword :dao.stream.apply/message string}}`, with additional envelope or error-map keys permitted ([apply.cljc:63](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:63), [apply.cljc:83](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:83), [apply.cljc:89](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:89)). |\ncollab/1790575143000-architect-one-envelope-ruling.gpt-6-sol.findings.md:24:**Errors.** Keep transport or client failures as rpc completion reasons unless a server is actually sending an application error response. Define distinct, stable apply-qualified reason words for `not-found`, `detached`, `ended`, `no-surface`, `oversize`, and `transport-error`. Map remote `no-surface` to `:dao.stream.apply/no-surface` and remote `oversize` to `:dao.stream.apply/oversize`; do not collapse them into the generic transport reason. `no-surface`, `oversize`, `not-found`, and `ended` are terminal for that binding; `detached` alone permits rebind; a generic transport failure follows the existing terminal path. The current mapping distinguishes only `not-found` and `channel-gone`, while `rebind` already accepts only `detached` ([rpc.cljc:432](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:432), [rpc.cljc:445](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:445), [rpc.cljc:568](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:568)). The remote source already emits separate `no-surface` and `oversize` protocol errors ([remote.cljc:39](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:39), [remote.cljc:123](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:123), [remote.cljc:164](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:164)).\ncollab/1790575143000-architect-one-envelope-ruling.gpt-6-sol.findings.md:34:“VM FFI call answered remotely, end-to-end” is accepted only when a real VM call emits its original apply request; the remote serving composition exposes its call-out request stream to an rpc-driven responder; that responder reads the **unchanged** request, appends a valid apply success or error to the response stream; and the VM resumes or raises the corresponding error through `yin.vm.ffi/call-result`, with the same ID and any extra request keys preserved where the request is retained or transported. Verify pending append retries, response `full`, gap, detach and rebind, terminal not-found, and UCF lift/lower of a retained call. The VM already constructs apply requests and checks apply response correlation ([semantic.cljc:467](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:467), [ffi.cljc:88](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc:88)); UCF already carries a retained request envelope verbatim ([ucf/remote.cljc:328](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc:328)).\ncollab/1790575143000-architect-one-envelope-ruling.gpt-6-sol.findings.md:44:Do **not** rename yin’s `dao.stream.apply/call` syntax or AST: that name is parsed and used throughout the VM ([yin.vm.cljc:194](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:194), [semantic.cljc:552](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:552)). Do **not** rebuild VM requests from op and args, tighten apply’s open predicates to rpc’s allocation domain, or add rpc event fields to the application envelope. The VM already emits apply requests, the apply protocol admits additional keys, and UCF explicitly retains the whole envelope because rebuilding it loses those keys ([semantic.cljc:467](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:467), [apply.cljc:38](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:38), [ucf/remote.cljc:328](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc:328)).\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:60:| `cursor`     | handle, anchor         | cursor outcome     |\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:91:descriptor's properties, the result keyword namespace, the anchor set — it is\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:142:must wait — a thread per pending operation, held for its duration — and it\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:147:arrives. Nothing waits, so one thread carries many pending operations: this\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:463:action a boundary adapter takes beyond appending, and it is not a judgement\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:501:- Cursors are minted by the stream: `cursor` on a handle takes an **anchor**\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:503:  defines two anchors — `:dao.stream/oldest`, the earliest retained\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:507:  transport-owned anchors (a byte offset, a timestamp). An anchor that is\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:509:  `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`. On a handle whose attachment has closed,\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:518:  `:dao.stream/cursor`), `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`,\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:526:  completeness.** The anchor names the earliest *retained* position, which on\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:530:  the cursor. Retained history is not complete history, and no anchor can make\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:532:  declared retention (see *Retention and Gaps*), never from an anchor.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:765:suffix. Everything else follows. Its `:oldest` anchor stays at the origin, so\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:771:returns `full` on reaching it, without appending and without evicting: refusing\ndocs/design/dao.stream.md:825:- **`seek`** — every valid cursor comes from the stream; anchored minting and\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/adapter.cljc:114:    :dao.stream.rpc/pending-request :yin.repl.adapter/pending-request\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/caller.cljc:18:   requests it sends afterwards. `step` yields `::pending` on retry or\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/caller.cljc:30:   local, and it releases the reflection's outstanding and pending work.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/caller.cljc:98:   Returns the caller value, `::status ::pending`, or\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/caller.cljc:115:          {::status ::pending,\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/caller.cljc:129:   leaves it `::pending`, or `::exhausted` once `::max-attempts` asks\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/caller.cljc:132:   (`close!`). A caller that is not pending is returned unchanged.\"\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/caller.cljc:134:  (if-not (= ::pending (::status caller))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/caller.cljc:136:    (let [r (stream/cursor (::call-out caller) stream/anchor-newest)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/responder.cljc:22:   `step` is one `dao.stream.apply/serve-once!` over the caller-owned\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/responder.cljc:34:     `:dao.stream.apply/ended` loss (`yin.vm.ffi/response-lost`)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/responder.cljc:112:   `::endpoint` is the `dao.stream.apply/endpoint` of the two remote\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/responder.cljc:159:   append (`dao.stream.apply/serve-once!`). Returns serve-once!'s own\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/responder.cljc:160:   result -- `:dao.stream.apply/outcome` and its extra keys -- with the\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/responder.cljc:167:   terminal, every step answers `:dao.stream.apply/terminal` and\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/responder.cljc:175:      (with {:dao.stream.apply/outcome :dao.stream.apply/terminal} s)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/responder.cljc:178:      (with {:dao.stream.apply/outcome ::retired}\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/responder.cljc:184:            s' (:dao.stream.apply/state result)]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/responder.cljc:185:        (if (= :dao.stream.apply/gap (:dao.stream.apply/outcome result))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/responder.cljc:187:              (with (assoc result :dao.stream.apply/outcome ::request-lost)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/holder.cljc:23:   settles every renewal still pending here as unknown: no bound\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/holder.cljc:146:           ::pending []\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/holder.cljc:176:  (= :dao.stream.remote/not-found (:dao.stream.remote/reason outcome)))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/holder.cljc:247:  \"Settle pending renewals from the event writer: an outcome naming the\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/holder.cljc:248:   renewal identity settles the oldest pending one; append-unknown\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/holder.cljc:264:              (= :dao.stream.remote/append-unknown\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/holder.cljc:265:                 (:dao.stream.remote/event v))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/holder.cljc:266:              (recur (-> (reduce #(observe %1 %2 failed) h (::pending h))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/holder.cljc:267:                         (assoc ::pending []))\nsrc/cljc/yin/v