{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -l ':dao\\\\.stream\\\\.apply/(detached|ended|not-found|no-surface|oversize|transport-error)' src test; rg -n 'apply|rpc|framebuffer' docs/design/dao.stream.md | head -80; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/repl/driver.cljc | sed -n '190,235p;350,420p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/repl/connect.cljc | sed -n '475,565p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc | sed -n '90,115p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/responder.cljc | sed -n '20,42p;175,202p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/observe.cljc | sed -n '1,125p'\"","aggregated_output":"test/yin/vm/ffi_test.cljc\ntest/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/responder_test.cljc\ntest/yin/repl/serve_connect_wire_test.clj\ntest/yin/repl/adapter_test.cljc\ntest/yin/repl/connect_test.cljc\ntest/dao/stream/rpc_test.cljc\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/connect.cljc\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/driver.cljc\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/responder.cljc\n923:  `abandon` over `dao.stream.rpc`. This is the third model, and the only one of\n970:`forward-step` and `rpc/poll!` already have. And `hydrate-async` is either\n   190\t  \"True while the RPC client holds an allocated request it has not managed to\n   191\t   append.  That request, not the driver's own record of the line, is what owns\n   192\t   the request path: `rpc/request!` retries it and ignores any new operation.\"\n   193\t  [state]\n   194\t  (rpc/unsent? (rpc-client state)))\n   195\t\n   196\t\n   197\t(defn- response-cursor-unminted?\n   198\t  \"True while the RPC client's response cursor is still the `newest` anchor\n   199\t   `connect/open` composed it with.  Over a remote reflection the anchor is\n   200\t   resolved by the server when the mint request reaches it, so a request that\n   201\t   crosses first can be answered at a position the resolved cursor already\n   202\t   lies past: that answer is never read.  A request must not be sent until\n   203\t   this is false.\"\n   204\t  [state]\n   205\t  (contains? stream/standard-anchors (:cursor (rpc-client state))))\n   206\t\n   207\t\n   208\t(defn pending-write?\n   209\t  \"True while a remote write the operator is owed remains in flight: a\n   210\t   request awaiting its response, a line held for a full writer, or an\n   211\t   envelope the RPC client still retains. This is one half of the tick\n   212\t   owner's `moved?` — a pending write must never wait out a backoff ceiling,\n   213\t   so cadence stays at the base interval until it clears.\"\n   214\t  [state]\n   215\t  (boolean (or (:outstanding state)\n   216\t               (:retrying state)\n   217\t               (remote-unsent? state)\n   218\t               (and (seq (:queued state)) (response-cursor-unminted? state)))))\n   219\t\n   220\t\n   221\t(def queueable-terminals\n   222\t  \"Terminal reasons after which an ordinary line still has a reattachment\n   223\t   decision to wait for.  Only `/detached` reattaches.  A reachability failure\n   224\t   may succeed through a *fresh* connection, but that connection cannot inherit\n   225\t   this binding's queued lines, so promising to deliver them would be false.\n   226\t   Every other terminal returns input to the local shell.\"\n   227\t  #{:dao.stream.apply/detached})\n   228\t\n   229\t\n   230\t(defn- remote-routed?\n   231\t  \"True while ordinary input belongs to the remote shell.  An operator\n   232\t   `(disconnect)` ends that immediately — the shell is local again from the next\n   233\t   line, without waiting for the terminal event the boundary will deposit — while\n   234\t   an uninvited drop keeps routing so the input can queue for a reattachment.  A\n   235\t   terminal with no reattachment behind it ends it too.\"\n   350\t\n   351\t(defn- connect-command\n   352\t  \"`(connect \\\"url\\\")`.  Reattaching a dropped connection keeps its medium,\n   353\t   cursor, and id allocator; connecting a fresh URL composes a new boundary.\"\n   354\t  [state line]\n   355\t  (let [url (second (adapter/command-form line))]\n   356\t    (cond\n   357\t      (not (string? url))\n   358\t      (publish state :yin.repl.driver/notice connect-usage)\n   359\t\n   360\t      (and (:connection state)\n   361\t           (= url (:url (:connection state)))\n   362\t           (connect/reattachable? (rpc-client state)))\n   363\t      (reattach state url)\n   364\t\n   365\t      ;; The operator's own disconnection is in flight: the terminal fact has\n   366\t      ;; not landed yet, so there is nothing to reattach to and nothing to\n   367\t      ;; refuse.  Composing a second boundary here would abandon the first.\n   368\t      ;; Once a terminal has landed the boundary has said everything it will\n   369\t      ;; ever say, so this must not answer for a non-reattachable one — that\n   370\t      ;; would trap the operator on a connection nothing can revive.\n   371\t      (and (:connection state)\n   372\t           (connect/operator-detached? (:connection state))\n   373\t           (nil? (remote-terminal state)))\n   374\t      (publish state :yin.repl.driver/notice\n   375\t               (str \"Still disconnecting from \" (:url (:connection state))\n   376\t                    \"; (connect …) reattaches once the boundary reports it\"))\n   377\t\n   378\t      (and (remote-routed? state) (not (remote-terminal state)))\n   379\t      (publish state :yin.repl.driver/notice\n   380\t               (str \"Already connected to \"\n   381\t                    (or (:url (:connection state)) \"the remote shell\")\n   382\t                    \"; (disconnect) first\"))\n   383\t\n   384\t      :else (open-connection state url))))\n   385\t\n   386\t\n   387\t(defn- submit-remote\n   388\t  [state line]\n   389\t  (let [result (adapter/submit-input (:adapter state) line)\n   390\t        state (assoc state :adapter (:yin.repl.adapter/state result))]\n   391\t    (case (:yin.repl.adapter/outcome result)\n   392\t      :yin.repl.adapter/requested\n   393\t      (assoc state\n   394\t             :outstanding (get-in result [:yin.repl.adapter/rpc-result\n   395\t                                          :dao.stream.rpc/id])\n   396\t             :retrying nil)\n   397\t\n   398\t      ;; The request was allocated but the writer answered `full`.  The same\n   399\t      ;; encoded request, with the same id, is retried on a later step.\n   400\t      :yin.repl.adapter/pending-request\n   401\t      (assoc state :retrying line)\n   402\t\n   403\t      (-> state\n   404\t          (assoc :retrying nil)\n   405\t          (publish :yin.repl.driver/notice\n   406\t                   (str \";; request not sent: \"\n   407\t                        (name (:yin.repl.adapter/outcome result))))))))\n   408\t\n   409\t\n   410\t(defn- handle-line\n   411\t  [state line]\n   412\t  (let [command (adapter/local-command line)]\n   413\t    (cond\n   414\t      ;; A shell that has quit evaluates nothing more.  Hosts append an\n   415\t      ;; end-of-input `(quit)` that a typed `(quit)` has already answered.\n   416\t      (not (:running? state)) state\n   417\t\n   418\t      (not (string? line))\n   419\t      (publish state :yin.repl.driver/diagnostic\n   420\t               (str \";; ignored non-string input: \"\n   475\t  (= :operator (:detached-by connection)))\n   476\t\n   477\t\n   478\t(defn reattachable?\n   479\t  \"True when the RPC client's terminal reason is the one reconnectable one.\"\n   480\t  [client]\n   481\t  (= :dao.stream.apply/detached (:terminal client)))\n   482\t\n   483\t\n   484\t(defn reattach\n   485\t  \"Reattach an existing connection: a fresh dial through the same host\n   486\t   adapter, both reflections attached again, then `rpc/rebind`.\n   487\t\n   488\t   The RPC client's response cursor and its collision-checked random id\n   489\t   allocator survive; only the writer and reader reflections change.\"\n   490\t  [connection client]\n   491\t  (if-not (reattachable? client)\n   492\t    (failure :yin.repl.connect/not-reattachable\n   493\t             (str \"Only a dropped connection reattaches; this one ended as \"\n   494\t                  (pr-str (:terminal client))))\n   495\t    (let [{:keys [url channel host]} connection\n   496\t          dial (fresh-dial host)]\n   497\t      (if-not dial\n   498\t        (failure :yin.repl.connect/invalid-composition\n   499\t                 \"The traffic medium minted no cursor\")\n   500\t        (let [[requests-result answers-result] (attach-pair dial channel)]\n   501\t          (cond\n   502\t            (not= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome requests-result))\n   503\t            (failure :yin.repl.connect/attach-failed\n   504\t                     (attach-outcome-message url requests-result))\n   505\t\n   506\t            (not= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome answers-result))\n   507\t            (failure :yin.repl.connect/attach-failed\n   508\t                     (attach-outcome-message url answers-result))\n   509\t\n   510\t            :else\n   511\t            {outcome-key :yin.repl.connect/reattached\n   512\t             connection-key (assoc connection\n   513\t                                   :dial dial\n   514\t                                   :status :established\n   515\t                                   :detached-by nil)\n   516\t             client-key (rpc/rebind client\n   517\t                                    (:dao.stream/handle requests-result)\n   518\t                                    (:dao.stream/handle answers-result))}))))))\n   519\t\n   520\t\n   521\t;; =============================================================================\n   522\t;; Terminal status, observed from the RPC client\n   523\t;; =============================================================================\n   524\t\n   525\t(defn- event\n   526\t  [kind text]\n   527\t  {event-key kind text-key text})\n   528\t\n   529\t\n   530\t(defn- terminal-transition\n   531\t  [connection terminal]\n   532\t  (case terminal\n   533\t    :dao.stream.apply/detached\n   534\t    [:detached (event :yin.repl.connect/detached\n   535\t                      (str \"Disconnected from \" (:url connection)\n   536\t                           \"; the served stream is untouched, so (connect \"\n   537\t                           (pr-str (:url connection)) \") reattaches\"))]\n   538\t\n   539\t    :dao.stream.apply/ended\n   540\t    [:ended (event :yin.repl.connect/ended\n   541\t                   (str \"The stream served at \" (:url connection)\n   542\t                        \" ended; there is nothing to reattach to\"))]\n   543\t\n   544\t    :dao.stream.apply/not-found\n   545\t    [:not-found (event :yin.repl.connect/not-found\n   546\t                       (str \"No stream is served at \"\n   547\t                            (:ws/path (:channel connection))\n   548\t                            \": the endpoint disclaimed it, so this is not \"\n   549\t                            \"retried\"))]\n   550\t\n   551\t    :dao.stream.apply/transport-error\n   552\t    [:transport-error\n   553\t     (event :yin.repl.connect/transport-error\n   554\t            (str \"Could not reach \" (:url connection)\n   555\t                 \"; this is a reachability failure and may succeed on retry\"))]\n   556\t\n   557\t    nil))\n   558\t\n   559\t\n   560\t(defn observe-terminal\n   561\t  \"Given `connection` and the RPC client's current `:terminal`, return\n   562\t   `[connection event]`.  The first terminal fact is the binding's\n   563\t   conclusion, published once: a connection already at a terminal status is\n   564\t   left alone, since a close/error race may repeat the same terminal on a\n   565\t   later poll.\"\n    90\t\n    91\t(def response-loss-statuses\n    92\t  \"The wake statuses of an FFI response reader whose response stream\n    93\t   can no longer answer it: it ended, or it lost values to a gap.\"\n    94\t  #{::response-ended ::response-gap})\n    95\t\n    96\t\n    97\t(defn response-lost\n    98\t  \"The portable apply error for a call whose response stream ended\n    99\t   (`::response-ended`) or skipped over evicted values (`::response-gap`)\n   100\t   before this call was answered. Both carry the terminal code\n   101\t   `:dao.stream.apply/ended`; `::loss` tells them apart. The caller\n   102\t   reports only the loss it observed -- it cannot know why the responder\n   103\t   closed the stream or what the gap held.\"\n   104\t  [call-id status]\n   105\t  (let [gap? (= ::response-gap status)]\n   106\t    {:call-id call-id,\n   107\t     :error {:dao.stream.apply/code :dao.stream.apply/ended,\n   108\t             :dao.stream.apply/message\n   109\t             (if gap?\n   110\t               \"FFI response stream lost values to a gap before this call was answered\"\n   111\t               \"FFI response stream ended before this call was answered\"),\n   112\t             ::loss (if gap? :dao.stream/gap :dao.stream/end)}}))\n   113\t\n   114\t\n   115\t(defn throw-response-lost!\n    20\t   required and explicit.\n    21\t\n    22\t   `step` is one `dao.stream.apply/serve-once!` over the caller-owned\n    23\t   server state the responder value carries: the request is read\n    24\t   whole, as the VM appended it -- extra keys and all, never rebuilt\n    25\t   from op and args -- its id answered on call-out, and a computed\n    26\t   response retained with its successor across `full`, so no handler\n    27\t   runs twice. Two responder-level rules sit on top:\n    28\t\n    29\t   - A gap on the call-in cursor is terminal loss (as\n    30\t     `yin.vm.ffi/bridge-step` treats it locally): a request was evicted\n    31\t     and the parked call that made it could never be answered. The\n    32\t     responder reports `::request-lost` and closes call-out, so every\n    33\t     VM waiting on that pair reads end and raises the portable\n    34\t     `:dao.stream.apply/ended` loss (`yin.vm.ffi/response-lost`)\n    35\t     instead of parking forever.\n    36\t   - Once the binding no longer serves call-in or call-out -- retired,\n    37\t     closed, or reclaimed by its lease -- the responder is terminal\n    38\t     (`::retired`) and invokes no further handler for that tenure.\n    39\t\n    40\t   The responder is a plain value threaded by its drive owner; nothing\n    41\t   here loops, waits or reads a clock.\"\n    42\t  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]\n   175\t      (with {:dao.stream.apply/outcome :dao.stream.apply/terminal} s)\n   176\t\n   177\t      (not (still-served? r))\n   178\t      (with {:dao.stream.apply/outcome ::retired}\n   179\t            (assoc s :terminal ::retired))\n   180\t\n   181\t      :else\n   182\t      (let [result (apply2/serve-once! (::handlers r) (::call-in r)\n   183\t                                       (::call-out r) s)\n   184\t            s' (:dao.stream.apply/state result)]\n   185\t        (if (= :dao.stream.apply/gap (:dao.stream.apply/outcome result))\n   186\t          (do (close-call-out! r)\n   187\t              (with (assoc result :dao.stream.apply/outcome ::request-lost)\n   188\t                    (assoc s' :terminal ::request-lost)))\n   189\t          (with result s'))))))\n     1\t(ns dao.stream.observe\n     2\t  \"One observation step over a DaoStream reader: read a value, act on it, and\n     3\t   advance the cursor only if the act succeeded.\n     4\t\n     5\t   `step` owns no scheduler, loop, state, callback or policy.  It classifies\n     6\t   every outcome the contract declares as data and hands the caller what it\n     7\t   needs to decide: the successor cursor on progress, the recovery cursor on a\n     8\t   gap, the raw answer on a defect.  What to do about a gap, whether a defect\n     9\t   is fatal, how many values to take per turn, and what state to carry between\n    10\t   turns all belong to the caller.\n    11\t\n    12\t   The cursor advances in exactly one place -- after the effect has answered\n    13\t   `ok` -- so effect-before-commit is a property of this seam rather than a\n    14\t   rule each caller keeps.  An effect that throws propagates before any\n    15\t   successor exists, which gives a throwing effect the same guarantee for\n    16\t   free.\n    17\t\n    18\t   Callers are `dao.stream.forward` (effect: append to another stream),\n    19\t   `dao.stream.observer` (effect: load a program into a VM) and\n    20\t   `dao.jing` (effect: materialize content).  Each adds its own loop and its\n    21\t   own policy above this step.\n    22\t\n    23\t   The law every interpreter here obeys is that an element's cursor advances\n    24\t   exactly when its disposition has been durably recorded.  This step serves\n    25\t   the interpreters whose disposition is an external, refusable effect -- an\n    26\t   append, a load, a materialization -- where that means advancing only after\n    27\t   the effect answered ok.  Interpreters whose disposition is a local, total\n    28\t   state transition keep their own read loops: `dao.stream.rpc/poll!`,\n    29\t   `dao.stream.apply/serve-once!` and `yin.vm.engine`'s wait set thread\n    30\t   whole caller state under a budget with terminal short-circuits, and for\n    31\t   them advancing first and advancing after the commit are the same fact,\n    32\t   because no window exists in which one happened and the other did not.\n    33\t   `serve-once!` is the proof that this is one law and not two families: it\n    34\t   advances after delivery for a correlatable request, on the diagnostic for\n    35\t   an uncorrelatable one, and on terminal loss for an undeliverable response.\n    36\t\n    37\t   There is no ordering parameter here and none is needed.  A consumer that\n    38\t   wants an element consumed even when its interpretation is defective writes\n    39\t   a *total* effect -- one that classifies every element as data and so always\n    40\t   answers ok -- and gets consume-exactly-once through this same step.\"\n    41\t  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]))\n    42\t\n    43\t\n    44\t(defn- valid-or-transport-error\n    45\t  \"Fold a defective host answer into the contract's outcome algebra.\n    46\t\n    47\t   A conforming operation already returns one of the contract outcomes. An\n    48\t   answer that is not a well-formed outcome map -- a non-map, a missing or\n    49\t   unqualified outcome keyword, a declared outcome missing its required\n    50\t   keys -- folds into `transport-error` with the raw answer retained, so\n    51\t   this step's branches stay total over whatever a composition supplies.\n    52\t   A well-formed outcome map whose outcome lies outside the operation's\n    53\t   declared set is a newer contract, not a defect in this host's assembly:\n    54\t   it is preserved exactly as it arrived, and `step` classifies it as\n    55\t   `refused` (Result Convention, unrecognized outcomes).\"\n    56\t  [operation result]\n    57\t  (let [defect (stream/validate-outcome operation result)]\n    58\t    (if (or (nil? defect) (= :unauthorized-outcome (:error defect)))\n    59\t      result\n    60\t      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\n    61\t       :dao.stream/error :dao.stream/invalid-operation-result\n    62\t       :dao.stream/answer result})))\n    63\t\n    64\t\n    65\t(defn step\n    66\t  \"Read one value from `source` at `cursor` and run `effect` on it.\n    67\t\n    68\t   `effect` is `(fn [value] -> outcome-map)` answering with the writer outcome\n    69\t   set: `ok`, `full` (\\\"not yet\\\"), `invalid-value`, `closed`, `refused`, or\n    70\t   `transport-error`.  An answer that is not a well-formed outcome map is\n    71\t   classified as `transport-error` with the raw answer retained.  A\n    72\t   well-formed outcome outside the declared set is unrecognized -- a newer\n    73\t   contract, not a defect -- and is preserved as it arrived: on a read it\n    74\t   classifies as `:refused`, on an effect as `:failed`, exactly as\n    75\t   `refused` classifies.  An effect that throws propagates.\n    76\t\n    77\t   Returns a map carrying `:cursor` and `:read` (the raw read answer) in every\n    78\t   case:\n    79\t\n    80\t   | status     | when                              | cursor    | also        |\n    81\t   | ---------- | --------------------------------- | --------- | ----------- |\n    82\t   | `:advance` | read ok, effect ok                | successor | `:effect`   |\n    83\t   | `:retry`   | read blocked, or effect full      | unchanged | `:outcome`  |\n    84\t   | `:ended`   | read end                          | unchanged | `:outcome`  |\n    85\t   | `:gap`     | read gap                          | unchanged | `:recovery` |\n    86\t   | `:refused` | read refused or unrecognized;     | unchanged | `:outcome`  |\n    87\t   |            | nothing was observed              |           |             |\n    88\t   | `:defect`  | read cursor-mismatch,             | unchanged | `:outcome`  |\n    89\t   |            | invalid-cursor, transport-error,  |           |             |\n    90\t   |            | or malformed                      |           |             |\n    91\t   | `:failed`  | effect invalid-value, closed,     | unchanged | `:outcome`, |\n    92\t   |            | refused, transport-error,         |           | `:effect`   |\n    93\t   |            | unrecognized, or malformed        |           |             |\n    94\t\n    95\t   A `:retry` from the effect also carries `:effect`.  The cursor is the\n    96\t   successor on `:advance` and unchanged on every other status.  `refused`\n    97\t   is a policy answer, not a defect: nothing was observed and nothing was\n    98\t   recorded, and the cursor stays where it was.  An unrecognized outcome\n    99\t   answers the same: a newer contract is a refusal, never a defect.\"\n   100\t  [source cursor effect]\n   101\t  (let [read-result (valid-or-transport-error :next (stream/next source cursor))\n   102\t        read-outcome (:dao.stream/outcome read-result)]\n   103\t    (case read-outcome\n   104\t      :dao.stream/ok\n   105\t      (let [effect-result (valid-or-transport-error\n   106\t                            :append!\n   107\t                            (effect (:dao.stream/value read-result)))\n   108\t            effect-outcome (:dao.stream/outcome effect-result)]\n   109\t        (case effect-outcome\n   110\t          :dao.stream/ok {:status :advance\n   111\t                          :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor read-result)\n   112\t                          :read read-result\n   113\t                          :effect effect-result}\n   114\t          :dao.stream/full {:status :retry\n   115\t                            :cursor cursor\n   116\t                            :outcome effect-outcome\n   117\t                            :read read-result\n   118\t                            :effect effect-result}\n   119\t          {:status :failed\n   120\t           :cursor cursor\n   121\t           :outcome effect-outcome\n   122\t           :read read-result\n   123\t           :effect effect-result}))\n   124\t\n   125\t      :dao.stream/blocked {:status :retry\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
